q q .423529 .478431 .501961 rg BT/F4+0 8 Tf 59.53 46.8 TD (August 2026)Tj ET .137255 .156863 .172549 rg BT/F2+0 8.5 Tf 547.285 46.8 TD (2)Tj ET Q q 1 0 0 1 59.53 701.5 cm .078431 .094118 .105882 rg BT 1.3 Tc/F2+0 10.5 Tf 0 12 TD (ABOUT THIS PAPER)Tj ET Q q 1 0 0 1 59.53 595.1 cm .137255 .156863 .172549 rg BT/F4+0 10 Tf 0 96.4 TD (This is a synthesized analysis, not original empirical research. It draws on publicly available analyst)Tj 0 -15.199997 TD (forecasts, industry surveys, and corporate reporting from sources including IDC, Gartner, Deloitte, Citi,)Tj T* (Andreessen Horowitz, AnalyticsWeek, NVIDIA, the FinOps Foundation, the International Energy Agency,)Tj 0 -15.200001 TD (and others, listed in full in the references at the back. Numerical figures in this paper are forecasts,)Tj T* (estimates, or modeled projections from those sources, attributed in-line where they appear. Worked)Tj T* (examples, such as the hospital comparison in Part 3, are illustrative rather than measured outcomes;)Tj 0 -15.2 TD (actual token reductions and cost gaps vary by workflow complexity and deployment context.)Tj ET Q q 1 0 0 1 59.53 525.2 cm .137255 .156863 .172549 rg BT/F4+0 10 Tf 0 50.8 TD (This revision keeps the May 2026 argument substantially intact, with plainer language throughout. It)Tj 0 -15.200001 TD (carries one rename from the first printing: the paper itself, first published as After the First Lap. The tier's)Tj T* (name, Deterministic AI Infrastructure, stands; Part 6 sets out why, against the objections raised since May.)Tj 0 -15.2 TD (A section at the end covers what has changed between publication and mid-August 2026.)Tj ET Q q 1 0 0 1 59.53 487.6 cm .078431 .094118 .105882 rg BT 1.3 Tc/F2+0 10.5 Tf 0 12 TD (EXECUTIVE SUMMARY)Tj ET Q q 1 0 0 1 59.53 426.8 cm .137255 .156863 .172549 rg BT/F4+0 10 Tf 0 50.8 TD (IDC forecasts worldwide AI spending of $301 billion in 2026, and broader market definitions place the)Tj 0 -15.200001 TD (figure higher. The investment is real, the productivity gains are real, and the strategic urgency driving the)Tj T* (spend is justified. What is also real, and increasingly visible to the CFOs whose budgets fund it, is that the)Tj 0 -15.2 TD (cost of running AI in production is now growing faster than the cost of buying it.)Tj ET Q q 1 0 0 1 59.53 341.7 cm .137255 .156863 .172549 rg BT/F4+0 10 Tf 0 66 TD (According to industry reporting collected by Andreessen Horowitz, some Fortune 500 companies now)Tj 0 -15.200001 TD (report monthly AI inference bills in the tens of millions of dollars. Analyst estimates of inference's share of)Tj T* (enterprise AI compute spend range from roughly two-thirds \(Deloitte\) to as high as eighty-five percent)Tj T* (\(AnalyticsWeek\). Forty-two percent of enterprises name workflow optimization as their top AI spending)Tj 0 -15.2 TD (priority for 2026, overtaking expansion for the first time.)Tj ET Q q 1 0 0 1 59.53 238.6 cm .054902 .227451 .266667 rg BT/F2+0 21 Tf 0 62 TD ($301B)Tj ET .333333 .384314 .407843 rg BT/F4+0 8.5 Tf 0 46 TD (IDC-forecast worldwide AI spending)Tj ET BT/F4+0 8.5 Tf 0 35 TD (in 2026; broader definitions \(Gartner\))Tj ET BT/F4+0 8.5 Tf 0 24 TD (place 2026 spending higher.)Tj ET .054902 .227451 .266667 rg BT/F2+0 21 Tf 168.6 62 TD (2/3 to 85%)Tj ET .333333 .384314 .407843 rg BT/F4+0 8.5 Tf 168.6 46 TD (Range of analyst estimates for)Tj ET BT/F4+0 8.5 Tf 168.6 35 TD (inference's share of enterprise AI)Tj ET BT/F4+0 8.5 Tf 168.6 24 TD (compute spend.)Tj ET .054902 .227451 .266667 rg BT/F2+0 21 Tf 337.1 62 TD (31% to 63%)Tj ET .333333 .384314 .407843 rg BT/F4+0 8.5 Tf 337.1 46 TD (Rise in organizations reporting AI as)Tj ET BT/F4+0 8.5 Tf 337.1 35 TD (an active FinOps concern, 2024 to)Tj ET BT/F4+0 8.5 Tf 337.1 24 TD (2025.)Tj ET .866667 .890196 .894118 RG .7 w n 158 8 m 158 80 l S n 326.5 8 m 326.5 80 l S Q q 1 0 0 1 59.53 91.8 cm .137255 .156863 .172549 rg BT/F4+0 10 Tf 0 126.8 TD (This paper argues that a structural reckoning is now underway. The first lap of enterprise AI was defined)Tj 0 -15.199997 TD (by three conditions running together: urgency to adopt, real and measurable productivity gains, and)Tj T* (relative affordability. Pricing was calibrated to drive usage, and that affordability is what made the urgency)Tj T* (rational. The second lap, already beginning, will be different. Affordability is ending, urgency persists, and)Tj T* (the productivity gains are now embedded in operations that enterprises cannot easily wind down. Boards)Tj 0 -15.200001 TD (are starting to ask what every dollar of AI spend produces, and that scrutiny matches the FinOps adoption)Tj T* (data showing AI cost concern roughly doubling year over year. Companies that cannot answer the)Tj T* (question face a forced choice between rationing AI use, raising prices, cutting headcount further, or)Tj 0 -15.2 TD (absorbing costs they cannot sustain.)Tj ET Q Q
