q q .423529 .478431 .501961 rg BT/F4+0 8 Tf 59.53 46.8 TD (August 2026)Tj ET .137255 .156863 .172549 rg BT/F2+0 8.5 Tf 547.0725 46.8 TD (3)Tj ET Q q 1 0 0 1 59.53 602.4 cm .137255 .156863 .172549 rg BT/F4+0 10 Tf 0 111.6 TD (Behind this reckoning is a more specific dynamic: a structural dependency on early-market foundation)Tj 0 -15.199997 TD (model pricing is taking shape inside enterprise operations. Current per-token prices are calibrated to drive)Tj T* (adoption, and may not reflect the long-run, fully loaded economics of providing frontier models. As)Tj T* (workflows become load-bearing for production operations, switching costs rise sharply. Foundation model)Tj 0 -15.200001 TD (labs face their own real economics: capital spending on data centers, energy, talent, and eventually)Tj T* (investor expectations of returns. Most analysts therefore expect pricing to drift toward sustainable levels)Tj T* (over time. This is not a criticism of the labs. It is the normal arc of an infrastructure sector maturing. The)Tj 0 -15.2 TD (exposure it creates for end-user enterprises, however, is real and worth planning for now rather than later.)Tj ET Q q 1 0 0 1 59.53 517.3 cm .137255 .156863 .172549 rg BT/F4+0 10 Tf 0 66 TD (The companies best positioned for the second wave are not the foundation model labs, and not the)Tj 0 -15.200001 TD (end-user enterprises trying to build AI capability internally. They are a third category: intermediaries whose)Tj T* (engineering encodes the structured, predictable work in deterministic code and invokes large language)Tj T* (models where their judgment adds value. This category needs a name. This paper proposes one:)Tj 0 -15.2 TD (Deterministic AI Infrastructure \(DAII\).)Tj ET Q Q
