q q .423529 .478431 .501961 rg BT/F4+0 8 Tf 59.53 46.8 TD (August 2026)Tj ET .137255 .156863 .172549 rg BT/F2+0 8.5 Tf 546.911 46.8 TD (4)Tj ET Q q 1 0 0 1 59.53 711 cm .078431 .094118 .105882 rg BT/F3+0 11 Tf 0 2 TD (1.)Tj ET Q q 1 0 0 1 59.53 685.5 cm .062745 .078431 .090196 rg BT/F2+0 20.5 Tf 0 4 TD (The Numbers Are Becoming Impossible to Ignore)Tj ET Q q 1 0 0 1 59.53 676.8 cm .866667 .890196 .894118 RG 1 J .7 w n 0 0 m 492.94 0 l S Q q 1 0 0 1 59.53 602 cm .137255 .156863 .172549 rg BT/F4+0 10 Tf 0 50.8 TD (The first lap of enterprise AI ran on three conditions in alignment: adoption pressure that made urgency)Tj 0 -15.200001 TD (rational, productivity gains that made spending justifiable, and pricing that made the economics tolerable.)Tj T* (The first two conditions remain. The third is changing. The numbers in this section establish the scale and)Tj 0 -15.2 TD (direction of that change.)Tj ET Q q 1 0 0 1 59.53 569.9 cm .078431 .094118 .105882 rg BT/F2+0 11.6 Tf 0 2.9 TD (Adoption has crossed the tipping point)Tj ET Q q 1 0 0 1 59.53 501.4 cm .137255 .156863 .172549 rg BT/F4+0 10 Tf 0 50.8 TD (Enterprise AI is no longer experimental. Sixty-four percent of organizations report actively using AI in)Tj 0 -15.200001 TD (operations, and seventy percent in North America. Eighty-six percent of enterprises increased their AI)Tj T* (budgets in 2026. The number of organizations with at least forty percent of AI projects in production is set)Tj 0 -15.2 TD (to double within six months. The pilots have become deployments.)Tj ET Q q 1 0 0 1 59.53 325.1 cm .137255 .156863 .172549 rg BT/F4+0 10 Tf 0 157.2 TD (Spending follows. IDC's worldwide AI spending guide forecasts $301 billion in 2026. Broader market)Tj 0 -15.199997 TD (definitions place the figure higher: Gartner's 2026 worldwide AI spending forecast reaches $2.5 trillion,)Tj T* (and Citi projects the total AI market above $4.2 trillion by 2030 with enterprise AI roughly half of that.)Tj T* (These figures measure different things. IDC tracks AI-centric systems and software narrowly, while)Tj T* (Gartner's broader definition includes adjacent IT infrastructure and services attributable to AI workloads.)Tj T* (The numbers are not directly comparable, but the direction across methodologies is the same: sharply up.)Tj T* (According to NVIDIA's State of AI Report 2026, the hyperscalers were collectively forecast to exceed)Tj -.03875 Tw 0 -15.200001 TD ($600 billion in capital expenditure in 2026, with roughly $450 billion tied directly to AI infrastructure. \(Their)Tj 0 Tw T* (own second-quarter guidance has since moved well past that; see the update at the end of this paper.\))Tj T* (Gartner projects that data center systems spending will jump 55.8 percent in 2026, the largest single)Tj 0 -15.2 TD (growth category in enterprise IT.)Tj ET Q q 1 0 0 1 59.53 293 cm .078431 .094118 .105882 rg BT/F2+0 11.6 Tf 0 2.9 TD (Inference has replaced training as the cost driver)Tj ET Q q 1 0 0 1 59.53 148.5 cm .137255 .156863 .172549 rg BT/F4+0 10 Tf 0 126.8 TD (Two activities consume AI compute. Training is the one-time work of building a model in the first place.)Tj 0 -15.199997 TD (Inference is the recurring work of running that model every time someone uses it. Public attention has)Tj T* (focused on training, because the costs of frontier model development make headlines. The shift in)Tj T* (enterprise AI economics, however, is happening on the inference side, where the cost compounds with)Tj T* (every query, every workflow, every deployment. Estimates vary on the exact share. Deloitte expects)Tj 0 -15.200001 TD (inference to account for roughly two-thirds of all AI compute in 2026, while AnalyticsWeek puts inference)Tj T* (as high as eighty-five percent of the enterprise AI budget. The direction is the same across estimates: the)Tj T* (cost story has moved from training to inference. By 2026, many enterprise AI cost models had crossed)Tj 0 -15.2 TD (into inference-dominant economics, a shift some analysts have called the Inference Flip.)Tj ET Q Q
