q q .423529 .478431 .501961 rg BT/F4+0 8 Tf 59.53 46.8 TD (August 2026)Tj ET .137255 .156863 .172549 rg BT/F2+0 8.5 Tf 546.911 46.8 TD (9)Tj ET Q q 1 0 0 1 59.53 500.9212 cm .290196 .333333 .352941 rg BT/F5+0 8.3 Tf 0 26.2 TD (Figure 3.)Tj .411765 .462745 .486275 rg/F6+0 8.3 Tf ( The spectrum of cost-discipline practices. Each practice intervenes at a different layer of the AI consumption stack,)Tj 0 -11.5 TD (from tightening individual calls to changing what the LLM is asked to do in the first place. The practices are complementary;)Tj T* (mature enterprise AI use will combine them.)Tj ET Q q 1 0 0 1 59.53 468.8212 cm .078431 .094118 .105882 rg BT/F2+0 11.6 Tf 0 2.9 TD (Why the cost-governance layer must sit elsewhere)Tj ET Q q 1 0 0 1 59.53 369.9212 cm .137255 .156863 .172549 rg BT/F4+0 10 Tf 0 81.2 TD (Current foundation model pricing reflects a specific moment in market formation. Per-token prices are)Tj 0 -15.199997 TD (heavily shaped by adoption incentives and intense competition, and may not reflect the full, long-run)Tj 0 -15.200001 TD (economics of operating frontier-scale infrastructure. The arrangement is sustained in part by venture)Tj T* (capital, hyperscaler cross-subsidies, and growth-stage investor tolerance for losses. None of this is)Tj T* (malpractice; it is the normal early-market pricing pattern for capital-intensive infrastructure. Cloud)Tj 0 -15.2 TD (computing, telecom, and electric utilities all passed through equivalent phases.)Tj ET Q q 1 0 0 1 59.53 224.0212 cm .137255 .156863 .172549 rg BT/F4+0 10 Tf 0 126.8 TD (What follows next is also normal: pricing tends to drift toward sustainable margin over time. Foundation)Tj 0 -15.199997 TD (labs face their own real economics: capital spending on data center buildout, energy costs that move with)Tj T* (global commodity markets, talent costs that compound with capability competition, and eventually investor)Tj T* (expectations of returns matching the capital deployed. None of these costs trend downward in a)Tj T* (coordinated way, and none of them are negotiable by foundation labs in any structural sense. Most)Tj 0 -15.200001 TD (analysts therefore expect costs to be passed through to customers over time, as they typically are in)Tj T* (capital-intensive infrastructure categories. Even if per-token prices continue falling, enterprises remain)Tj T* (exposed if production token volumes and workflow dependence grow faster than unit-cost reductions,)Tj 0 -15.2 TD (which the consumption multipliers from agentic workflows make likely.)Tj ET Q q 1 0 0 1 59.53 78.12123 cm .137255 .156863 .172549 rg BT/F4+0 10 Tf 0 126.8 TD (The exposure this creates for end-user enterprises is the specific combination that makes early-market)Tj 0 -15.199997 TD (infrastructure pricing dangerous: real urgency to adopt \(competitive pressure\), real value \(the productivity)Tj T* (gains are genuine\), and engineered affordability \(pricing that may not reflect the long-run, fully loaded)Tj T* (economics of frontier-model provision\). Each factor alone is manageable. Together, they produce)Tj T* (structural dependency. By the time pricing normalizes, AI workflows will be load-bearing for operations)Tj 0 -15.200001 TD (that enterprises cannot easily wind down. Switching to a different foundation lab solves nothing if all)Tj T* (foundation labs face the same eventual cost normalization. Even modest per-token price increases will)Tj T* (land hard on enterprises whose token volumes are now measured in production scale rather than pilot)Tj 0 -15.2 TD (scale. The risk is not that AI becomes unaffordable overnight. The risk is that enterprises allow AI)Tj ET Q Q /fzFrm0 Do
