q q .423529 .478431 .501961 rg BT/F4+0 8 Tf 59.53 46.8 TD (August 2026)Tj ET .137255 .156863 .172549 rg BT/F2+0 8.5 Tf 543.1965 46.8 TD (19)Tj ET Q q 1 0 0 1 59.53 693.6 cm .137255 .156863 .172549 rg BT/F4+0 10 Tf 0 20.4 TD (which the thesis can be tested, not as precise forecasts. The update section at the end of this paper)Tj 0 -15.2 TD (scores them against three more months of data.)Tj ET Q q 1 0 0 1 59.53 655.5 cm .078431 .094118 .105882 rg BT/F3+0 11 Tf 0 2 TD (5.)Tj ET Q q 1 0 0 1 59.53 630 cm .062745 .078431 .090196 rg BT/F2+0 20.5 Tf 0 4 TD (What Enterprise Buyers Should Measure Now)Tj ET Q q 1 0 0 1 59.53 621.3 cm .866667 .890196 .894118 RG 1 J .7 w n 0 0 m 492.94 0 l S Q q 1 0 0 1 59.53 485.7 cm .137255 .156863 .172549 rg BT/F4+0 10 Tf 0 111.6 TD (This section is for the CFO, head of procurement, or analyst about to buy AI software at scale. Most AI)Tj 0 -15.199997 TD (procurement conversations today are driven by product demos: vendors show what their tool can do, and)Tj T* (buyers decide whether to buy based on capability. That made sense in the first lap, when affordable)Tj -.039412 Tw T* (pricing made cost a secondary concern. In the second lap, the economics of running the product matter as)Tj -.013125 Tw 0 -15.200001 TD (much as the capability. The five questions below help a buyer distinguish vendors who have engineered for)Tj 0 Tw T* (durable, predictable economics from vendors whose pricing relies on conditions that may not last. Buyers)Tj T* (who ask these questions in 2026 will be ahead of their peers when these questions become standard)Tj 0 -15.2 TD (procurement practice.)Tj ET Q q 1 0 0 1 59.53 415.8 cm .137255 .156863 .172549 rg BT/F2+0 10 Tf 0 50.8 TD (1. What does one full run of your product actually cost you?)Tj/F4+0 10 Tf ( Not the API price quoted by the foundation)Tj 0 -15.200001 TD (lab: the full cost the vendor incurs each time a customer runs the workflow end to end. A vendor that)Tj T* (cannot give a concrete dollar figure has not engineered the product to a known cost. That is a signal that)Tj 0 -15.2 TD (pricing is being set by guesswork or by hope, not by economics that hold up at scale.)Tj ET Q q 1 0 0 1 59.53 330.7 cm .137255 .156863 .172549 rg BT -.034444 Tw/F2+0 10 Tf 0 66 TD (2. How much does cost vary from one run to the next?)Tj/F4+0 10 Tf ( Averages hide volatility. A workflow that averages)Tj 0 Tw 0 -15.200001 TD (50 cents per run but occasionally spikes to 50 dollars, because the underlying AI got stuck in a loop or)Tj T* (pulled in too much context, is not a workflow with predictable economics. Ask for the typical cost, the cost)Tj T* (on a bad day, and the cost on a worst-case day. \(In statistical terms: median, ninety-fifth percentile, and)Tj 0 -15.2 TD (ninety-ninth percentile.\) A vendor that tracks these has done the work. A vendor that doesn't has not.)Tj ET Q q 1 0 0 1 59.53 230.4 cm .137255 .156863 .172549 rg BT/F2+0 10 Tf 0 81.2 TD (3. What happens to your prices if your AI supplier raises theirs?)Tj/F4+0 10 Tf ( Much of foundation model pricing today)Tj 0 -15.199997 TD (is shaped by venture capital, by hyperscaler cross-subsidies, and by some frontier labs' willingness to)Tj 0 -15.200001 TD (operate at substantial losses to capture market share. Those conditions are unlikely to persist indefinitely.)Tj T* (A vendor whose product economics collapse if foundation model prices rise twenty percent has built their)Tj T* (business on someone else's unsustainable pricing. A vendor with engineered consumption will see margin)Tj 0 -15.2 TD (pressure under that scenario, but not margin collapse. Ask which one you are buying.)Tj ET Q q 1 0 0 1 59.53 130.1 cm .137255 .156863 .172549 rg BT/F2+0 10 Tf 0 81.2 TD (4. How much of the work is done by your code versus by the AI?)Tj/F4+0 10 Tf ( This is the question that separates)Tj 0 -15.199997 TD (engineering from prompt engineering. A vendor who can describe specifically which parts of the workflow)Tj 0 -15.200001 TD (run on structured code \(predictable, fast, cheap, repeatable\) and which parts call out to a large language)Tj T* (model \(powerful but expensive and variable\) is operating at the engineering tier this paper describes. A)Tj T* (vendor who waves the question off, or answers vaguely, has not done the structural work. The distinction)Tj 0 -15.2 TD (matters because it tells you whether the product's economics will hold up as your usage grows.)Tj ET Q q 1 0 0 1 59.53 75.4 cm .137255 .156863 .172549 rg BT/F2+0 10 Tf 0 35.6 TD (5. Show me how you check that the output is right.)Tj/F4+0 10 Tf ( Output that has not been checked against a known)Tj 0 -15.200001 TD (correct structure is output that may quietly be wrong. A vendor whose product simply ships whatever the)Tj 0 -15.2 TD (AI returns is selling demonstrations, not infrastructure. A mature AI product has a validation layer that)Tj ET Q Q
